\chapter*{Preface}
This book presents the most significant mathematical discoveries made between the years 2000 and 2025. It is designed for undergraduate students who have completed introductory courses in calculus, linear algebra, and basic proof techniques. Each chapter focuses on one landmark result, explaining the mathematical ideas, the context of the problem, and the impact on modern mathematics.

The selections are organized into 21 chapters, each covering one landmark discovery:

\begin{enumerate}
\item The Poincar\'e Conjecture — Perelman (2003)
\item Fermat's Last Theorem and the Modularity Theorem — Wiles (1993--1995)
\item The Kepler Conjecture — Hales (2005), formally verified by Flyspeck (2014)
\item Bounded Gaps Between Primes — Zhang (2013), Maynard, Tao
\item The Kervaire Invariant One Problem — Hill, Hopkins, Ravenel (2016)
\item Maryam Mirzakhani's Work on Hyperbolic Surfaces — Fields Medal 2014
\item Classification of Finite Simple Groups — completed 2004
\item Sphere Packing in Dimensions 8 and 24 — Viazovska (2016), Fields Medal 2022
\item The Fundamental Lemma — Ngo Bao Chau (2009), Fields Medal 2010
\item The Weak Goldbach Conjecture — Helfgott (2013)
\item The Erd\H{o}s Distinct Distances Problem — Guth and Katz (2010)
\item The Geometric Langlands Program — Gaitsgory, Ben-Zvi, Francis, Nadler (2010s)
\item The Virtual Haken Conjecture and Surface Subgroup Conjecture — Agol, Kahn, Markovic (2012)
\item The Green--Tao Theorem — Green and Tao (2004)
\item The AKS Primality Test — Agrawal, Kayal, Saxena (2002)
\item The Willmore and Lawson Conjectures — Marques, Neves, Brendle (2012--2013)
\item Catalan's Conjecture — Mihailescu (2002)
\item Modern Combinatorics Breakthroughs — Kahn, Kalai, Park, Pham, et al. (2012--2022)
\item Perfectoid Spaces and p-adic Geometry — Scholze (2011--2025)
\item The Sato-Tate Conjecture — Clozel, Harris, Taylor (2008--2011)
\item The Erd\H{o}s Discrepancy Problem — Tao (2015)
\item The Kadison--Singer Problem — Marcus, Spielman, Srivastava (2013)
\item Breakthroughs in Random Matrix Theory — universality results (2010s--2022)
\item The Kakeya Conjecture — Wang, Zahl, et al. (2020s)
\end{enumerate}

Each chapter is designed for independent study and includes prerequisites, exercises with hints, timelines, and citations for further reading.

\chapter*{How to Use This Book}
Each chapter follows a consistent structure:
\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Prerequisites} -- what you should know before reading
\item \textbf{The Problem} -- statement and historical background
\item \textbf{Mathematical Theory} -- definitions, key concepts, and previous results
\item \textbf{The Discovery} -- the breakthrough proof and its main ideas
\item \textbf{Impact} -- consequences and connections to other areas
\item \textbf{Exercises} -- problems to test understanding (with hints in appendix)
\item \textbf{Timeline} -- key dates in the history of the problem
\item \textbf{Citations} -- references for further study
\end{enumerate}

Exercises are marked by difficulty: \emph{Basic} (direct application), \emph{Intermediate} (requires synthesis), \emph{Challenge} (open-ended or research-level). Hints and solutions are provided in the appendix.

\chapter*{Acknowledgments}
This book draws on the work of countless mathematicians. Special thanks to the authors of the original papers cited throughout, and to the many expository articles and lecture notes that made these proofs accessible. The diagrams were created using SVG and converted to PDF for LaTeX compatibility.

